* /**
*  * @file GIT.OSFILE
*  * @version 1.0
*  */
* GIT.OSFILE(op, path, data, ok) — read or write a whole OS file.
*
*   op    "READ"  — data = the file's contents; ok = 0 if it does not exist
*         "WRITE" — write data, replacing whatever was there; ok = 0 on failure
*
* The second platform seam, for the same reason as GIT.OBJ: OSREAD and OSWRITE
* are UniData statements that UniVerse does not have at all — it parses
* `OSWRITE IG ON IGF` as a variable followed by a variable and refuses the file.
* So, as with CALLC, the UniData spelling must be excluded by the PREPROCESSOR
* rather than by a runtime branch, because the text still has to compile.
*
* The content is arbitrary bytes — the caller's is 0xFE-separated — so both arms
* move it whole and unaltered.  On UniVerse that means WRITEBLK/READBLK rather
* than the line-oriented WRITESEQ/READSEQ, which would be at the mercy of any
* newline in the data.
*
* Not needed on MVX: its callers reach the same state as a record in the account
* file, which is the native shape there.
      SUBROUTINE GIT.OSFILE(OP, PATH, DATA, OK)
      $INCLUDE BP.INC PLATFORM.H
      OK = 0
*
      $IFDEF UDT
      IF OP = "READ" THEN
         OSREAD DATA FROM PATH THEN OK = 1 ELSE DATA = ""
      END ELSE
         OSWRITE DATA ON PATH
         OK = 1
      END
      $ENDIF
*
      $IFDEF UV
      IF OP = "READ" THEN
         DATA = ""
         OPENSEQ PATH TO OSF THEN
*           Read to the end in chunks.  A regular file DOES short-return at EOF
*           (a FIFO does not — see GIT.RPC), so a short chunk means the end.
            LOOP
               CHUNK = ""
               READBLK CHUNK FROM OSF, 8192 THEN
                  DATA = DATA : CHUNK
                  IF LEN(CHUNK) < 8192 THEN EXIT
               END ELSE
                  EXIT
               END
            REPEAT
            CLOSESEQ OSF
            OK = 1
         END ELSE
            DATA = ""
         END
      END ELSE
         OPENSEQ PATH TO OSF ELSE
            CREATE OSF ELSE RETURN
         END
*        WEOFSEQ after the write truncates at the current position, so replacing
*        the contents with something SHORTER does not leave the old tail behind.
         WRITEBLK DATA TO OSF THEN OK = 1 ELSE OK = 0
         WEOFSEQ OSF
         CLOSESEQ OSF
      END
      $ENDIF
*
      RETURN
      END
